    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "After Chef Barinov bargains for half the cockroach betting pool, Denis tells Kostya that Katya stopped answering his calls, and Kostya uses his law background to suggest marriage. Listen to the Russian dialogue.",
      "gap": 0.8
    },
    {
      "v": "chef",
      "t": "Вы опять за старое?",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Шеф, мы только после работы.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Там же ставки. Хотите в долю?",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "chef",
      "t": "Ладно, только после работы.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Всё, она сильно обиделась.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Она на телефон не отвечает.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Как я должен был поступить?",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "kostya",
      "t": "Слушай меня как хороший юрист.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "kostya",
      "t": "Формально, да, ты её парень.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "kostya",
      "t": "Но юридически ты ей никто.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "kostya",
      "t": "Но один выход точно есть.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "kostya",
      "t": "Тебе надо на ней жениться.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "kostya",
      "t": "Ты же любишь Катю.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "chef",
      "t": "Рабочий день уже начался!",
      "gap": 0.9
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "Now let's go back through their conversation line by line and unpack the Russian phrasing.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "chef",
      "t": "Вы опять за старое?",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'Are you at it again?' Notice how Russian expresses 'you' and 'old' with crisp inflection rather than helper words. Denis answers right away.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Шеф, мы только после работы.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'Chef, we only do it after work.' Denis anchors the line on 'Chef Barinov', while 'job' completes the thought.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Там же ставки. Хотите в долю?",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'There are bets there. Do you want a cut?' The grammar packs 'there' and 'share' into a tight, idiomatic kitchen exchange.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "chef",
      "t": "Ладно, только после работы.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'Fine, only after work.' The meaning hinges on 'all right' working together with 'job' in spoken Russian. Denis answers right away.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Всё, она сильно обиделась.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'That is it, she got really offended.' Notice how Russian expresses 'everything' and 'got offended' with crisp inflection rather than helper words.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Она на телефон не отвечает.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'She is not answering her phone.' The meaning hinges on 'she' working together with 'is responsible' in spoken Russian.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Как я должен был поступить?",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'How was I supposed to act?' Notice how Russian expresses 'how' and 'to act' with crisp inflection rather than helper words. Kostya answers right away.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "kostya",
      "t": "Слушай меня как хороший юрист.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'Listen to me as a good lawyer.' Here the Russian phrasing highlights 'listen' before landing on 'lawyer'.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "kostya",
      "t": "Формально, да, ты её парень.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'Formally, yes, you are her boyfriend.' In Russian, the idea of 'formally' flows naturally into 'boyfriend' without extra filler words.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "kostya",
      "t": "Но юридически ты ей никто.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'But legally you are nobody to her.' Spoken Russian keeps this brisk, leaning on 'but' and finishing on 'nobody'.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "kostya",
      "t": "Но один выход точно есть.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'But there is definitely one solution.' The grammar packs 'but' and 'there is' into a tight, idiomatic kitchen exchange.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "kostya",
      "t": "Тебе надо на ней жениться.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'You need to marry her.' Here the Russian phrasing highlights 'to you' before landing on 'to get married'.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "kostya",
      "t": "Ты же любишь Катю.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'You love Katya, after all.' Notice how Russian expresses 'you' and 'proper name' with crisp inflection rather than helper words. Chef Barinov answers right away.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "chef",
      "t": "Рабочий день уже начался!",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'The workday has already started!' Notice how Russian expresses 'working' and 'started' with crisp inflection rather than helper words. Alright, let's hear the entire conversation one more time from start to finish.",
      "gap": 0.9
    },
